From fe2c47942e5e79c4f87084fdb60f57b1f6cb80e8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ObeseCatLord <6099645+ObeseCatLord@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 10:26:21 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] ee: thread-safe GS interrupt posting, kernel-RAM pool guards,
 panel IO sampling

Code review 55 items C8, C1, C2, C9, B4, B6, B7 and the debug-panel thread note.

- postEventAfterCycles reads the executor-private cycle clock only on the
  executor thread (m_executorThread is atomic now); any other thread, such as
  the GS interrupt sink after a GS render thread exists, queues the event for
  the next checkpoint and logs once.
- Stack and heap declarations inside the native SDK pools (0x20000-0x51000)
  are refused like the invocation-stack arena; SetupHeap starts a heap that
  would begin inside them after them. Test fixtures that put thread stacks
  there moved to free RAM.
- The invocation-stack arena warns when its last slot is keyed.
- submitDmaSend hands delayed DMAC causes to the scheduler.
- SetupThread keeps the segment of the argument block address in the argv
  pointers it writes.
- The disc image identity (a stat) is re-checked at most once per guest vsync;
  an armed changer says so when the current disc also holds the program of
  another registered disc instead of silently not switching.
- The debug panel's IO tab no longer reads PS2Memory from the UI thread (the
  register map races the game thread and reading a CHCR clears STR): the game
  thread samples the non-CHCR rows once per vsync through a scheduler hook.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
